  #pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=state complete dim=1
  #pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=state complete dim=2
  #pragma HLS latency min=0 max=0
  #pragma HLS pipeline II=1

  // Atomic read all source elements first
  const uint8_t src[12] = {
      state[0][1], state[1][1], state[2][1], state[3][1],  // Column 1
      state[0][2], state[1][2], state[2][2], state[3][2],  // Column 2
      state[0][3], state[1][3], state[2][3], state[3][3]   // Column 3
  };

  // Single-cycle parallel write pattern
  state[0][1] = src[1]; state[1][1] = src[2]; state[2][1] = src[3]; state[3][1] = src[0];
  state[0][2] = src[6]; state[2][2] = src[4]; state[1][2] = src[7]; state[3][2] = src[5];
  state[0][3] = src[11]; state[3][3] = src[10]; state[2][3] = src[9]; state[1][3] = src[8];
}

static void ShiftRows(state_t *state) {
  ShiftRows_hls(*state);
}
```

This optimization:
1. Uses explicit pipeline pragma with II=1 to force single-cycle execution
2. Flattens all source reads into a single array initialization
3. Performs all destination writes in one atomic block
4. Maintains full 2D array partitioning for parallel access
5. Uses index-based access pattern for better HLS pattern recognition

The flattened source array and explicit pipeline pragma provide the strongest possible hints to the HLS tool that:
- All reads must complete before any writes begin
- All writes should be scheduled in parallel
- The entire operation should complete in a single clock cycle

This represents the most aggressive latency-oriented optimization possible while maintaining functional correctness.
